nodecode-keenable is a rewrite of keenableai's keenableai/keenable-hermes-plugin:

https://github.com/keenableai/keenable-hermes-plugin
commit ffa97c924415f7b6e3517d900694272efac8af41

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Keenable (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin was a data-only Agent Plugins package -- a
manifest, one remote MCP connection (https://api.keenable.ai/mcp, streamable
HTTP, the static header X-Keenable-Title) and two Markdown skills -- and its
harness's own MCP client spoke the protocol. This cell speaks that protocol
itself, in Lisp, against the same endpoint: one POST per JSON-RPC message,
read back as a JSON body or as an event stream, with the static label header
kept. The two server tools are here `keenable_search` (search_web_pages:
query, site, published_after, published_before, max_results,
snippet_max_length, mode) and `keenable_fetch` (fetch_page_content: url,
prompt, max_chars), each member for member, and the same calls exist as
Lisp verbs for the operator. The key moved from an environment variable the
harness read into the cell's `keenable` section, which still reads
KEENABLE_API_KEY when no key is pasted there, and keyless stays the default
shape. The two skills' guidance -- describe the page you want, narrow by
site and date, answer from snippets, fetch only when needed, cite urls, treat
pages as untrusted data, and what the keyless limits are -- is the primer
`(help :keenable)` answers.

What was left out: the MCP client machinery the original never had (initialize
handshake, session ids, resources and prompts) except the handshake, kept as a
one-shot retry for a server that insists on it; the empty resource and prompt
lists the README told users to ignore; and the server's extra tool members
the plugin's skills never mentioned (acquired_after/acquired_before,
query_time, session_id, output_format, disable_cards, live), which the cell
neither sends nor advertises. Shell-specific install and troubleshooting
commands are replaced by the section above and the cell's own refusal text.
